{"text": "Some authors believe that collectives of individuals can be more intelligent than individuals taken singly, sometimes pointing to the example of ants, bees, and other social animals. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> It is certainly true that, by addressing a question to a collective crowd intelligence, you may get a good answer more easily than by, say, researching the literature or asking a philosopher. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But this works in a reliable fashion only where some genuine expert happens to be in the crowd. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> This is because human intelligence is an individual capability that is non additive. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Collectives can, certainly, carry out plans that individuals cannot ― such as building Cologne Cathedral. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Collectives can also evolve new social patterns over very long periods of time through trial and error or through the sort of transgenerational collaboration that is involved, for example, in the creation and sustainment of scientific disciplines. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But collectives do not write poetry or novels, discover new aspects of nature, or solve difficult mathematical problems. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> This is done predominantly by individuals or by very small groups.", "alignment": {"characters": ["S", "o", "m", "e", " ", "a", "u", "t", "h", "o", "r", "s", " ", "b", "e", "l", "i", "e", "v", "e", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", " ", "c", "o", "l", "l", "e", "c", "t", "i", "v", "e", "s", " ", "o", "f", " ", "i", "n", "d", "i", "v", "i", "d", "u", "a", "l", "s", " ", "c", "a", "n", " ", "b", "e", " ", "m", "o", "r", "e", " ", "i", "n", "t", "e", "l", "l", "i", "g", "e", "n", "t", " ", "t", "h", "a", "n", " ", "i", "n", "d", "i", "v", "i", "d", "u", "a", "l", "s", " ", "t", "a", "k", "e", "n", " ", "s", "i", "n", "g", "l", "y", ",", " ", "s", "o", "m", "e", "t", "i", "m", "e", "s", " ", "p", "o", "i", "n", "t", "i", "n", "g", " ", "t", "o", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "e", "x", "a", "m", "p"